}

// Has input, has return
int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

// Has input, no return
void printNum(int n) {
    printf("%d", n);
}

// No input, has return
int getMax() {
    return 100;
}

So both statements a and b are correct. Answer: c) Both a and b.

Correct Answer: c) printf("\\n")

Explanation:
In C strings, the backslash \ is an escape character. To print a literal backslash, you need to use \\ (double backslash).

- printf("\n")  → Prints a newline (moves to next line), does NOT print \n on screen.
- echo "\n"    → This is a shell/Python command, not valid in C.
- printf("\\n") → Prints the literal characters \ and n on screen. Output: \n

So to print \n literally on screen, use:
printf("\\n");

Example:
#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    printf("\\n");  // Output: \n  (printed on screen)
    printf("\n");   // Output: (newline - moves to next line)
    return 0;
}

So the correct answer is c) printf("\\n").

