- num % 11 == 0 checks if num is divisible by 11
- Both conditions together with && check divisibility by both

PART a: Difference between for and while loop (3 marks)

for loop:
- Used when the number of iterations is KNOWN in advance.
- Initialization, condition, and update are all in one line.
- More compact syntax.
Syntax:
for (initialization; condition; update) {
    // body
}

while loop:
- Used when the number of iterations is NOT known in advance.
- Initialization is done before the loop, update is inside the body.
- More flexible for condition-based loops.
Syntax:
initialization;
while (condition) {
    // body
    update;
}

Difference Table:
Feature         | for loop                   | while loop
Use case        | Known iterations           | Unknown iterations
Initialization  | Inside for statement       | Before loop
Update          | Inside for statement       | Inside body
Readability     | Compact                    | Slightly longer
Type            | Entry controlled           | Entry controlled

Example — Print 1 to 5:
// for loop
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);  // Output: 1 2 3 4 5
}

// while loop
int i = 1;
while (i <= 5) {
    printf("%d ", i);  // Output: 1 2 3 4 5
    i++;
}

