PART a: goto Statement (3 marks)

Definition:
The goto statement is an unconditional control transfer statement. It transfers program control to a specified labeled statement anywhere within the same function.

Syntax:
goto label_name;
...
label_name:
    // statements

Use Cases:
1. Jumping out of deeply nested loops (though break is preferred).
2. Error handling in some legacy C code.
3. Jumping to cleanup code at the end of a function.

Example:
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int i = 1;

loop:
    if (i <= 5) {
        printf("%d ", i);
        i++;
        goto loop;  // Jump back to label 'loop'
    }

    printf("\nDone\n");
    return 0;
}

Output: 1 2 3 4 5
Done

Why goto should be avoided:
- Makes code difficult to read and understand.
- Breaks structured programming principles.
- Can create spaghetti code (tangled control flow).
- Modern alternatives (break, continue, functions) are better.

