PART b: Array Definition and Advantages (1 + 2 marks)

What is an Array? (1 mark)
An array is a collection of elements of the same data type stored in contiguous memory locations, accessed by a common name and an index.
Syntax: data_type array_name[size];
Example: int marks[5] = {80, 75, 90, 85, 70};

Advantages of Array (2 marks):
1. Easy Access: Elements can be accessed randomly using their index in O(1) time.
   Example: marks[2] directly gives 90.

2. Memory Efficiency: Elements are stored in contiguous memory, making access faster.

3. Code Simplicity: Loops can process all elements without declaring separate variables for each.

4. Sorting and Searching: Arrays support efficient sorting and searching algorithms.

5. Represents Multiple Values: Stores large amounts of data under one name.

PART a: strcmp() and strcat() (3 marks)

(i) strcmp() — String Compare:
Purpose: Compares two strings lexicographically (character by character using ASCII values).
Header: <string.h>
Syntax: int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);

Return values:
- Returns 0  → if s1 == s2 (both strings are equal)
- Returns > 0 → if s1 > s2 (s1 comes after s2 lexicographically)
- Returns < 0 → if s1 < s2 (s1 comes before s2 lexicographically)

Example:
#include <string.h>
char s1[] = "Apple";
char s2[] = "Apple";
char s3[] = "Mango";
printf("%d", strcmp(s1, s2));  // Output: 0   (equal)
printf("%d", strcmp(s1, s3));  // Output: -ve (A < M)
printf("%d", strcmp(s3, s1));  // Output: +ve (M > A)

(ii) strcat() — String Concatenation:
Purpose: Appends (joins) the source string to the end of the destination string.
Header: <string.h>
Syntax: char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src);

Example:
#include <string.h>
char dest[30] = "Hello ";
char src[] = "World";
strcat(dest, src);
printf("%s", dest);  // Output: Hello World

Note: The destination array must be large enough to hold both strings.

